WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.145 --> 00:00:02.520
HUNTER WILLIAMS: So a little
bit of background on myself.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.460
I, as Bill mentioned, was on
the Google Cloud Platform team.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.130
I don't come from the
media and content side.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.810
I come from the cloud
technology side.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.940
So when Kevin reached out
to me earlier this year

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.400
and asked me to come and join
the team as they spun off

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.260
this business, I was
really excited to go

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:21.690
on to the buy side
of cloud computing

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:25.550
and see how things are
from the other side.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.180
So I joined about
two months ago.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.770
I came from the Google
Cloud Sales Team,

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:34.480
previously with RightScale.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.360
Most my focus in
that world had been

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.100
on media and entertainment,
gaming as well.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.190
So I knew a lot of the
people in the industry,

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:44.680
but it was really
exciting to get

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.610
to join an actual studio that
was building a cloud product.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.780
So I want to talk a little bit
about how Atomic Fiction uses

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.880
Conductor and then ultimately
how Conductor uses GCP.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.480
And to start, I want to talk
a little bit about the history

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:01.910
of Atomic Fiction.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:06.280
So in 2009, Kevin
Baillie and Ryan Tudhope

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.790
founded Atomic Fiction.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:12.480
They were alumni from IMD,
which is a Disney company

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.560
or was a Disney company.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.820
And they were also at
Lucas for quite some time.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:18.278
When they founded
the company, they

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:21.980
wanted to build a business
around new technologies that

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.550
had just come out.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:24.934
So they were using the cloud.

00:01:24.934 --> 00:01:26.600
At the time they were
using Zync Render,

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.431
as Todd talked about earlier.

00:01:29.431 --> 00:01:31.180
But as the company
grew, and now that it's

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:35.730
125 employees, two locations--
well, three locations,

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:39.880
I guess, if you count
the LA office-- the needs

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:40.960
of the business changed.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.820
And so they decided, as
many different studios do,

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.579
to build their own
render pipeline.

00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:47.620
But they were going to
build this render pipeline

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.190
in the cloud.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:50.980
So enter Conductor.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.630
They hired a guy
from DreamWorks who

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.885
had built very large-scale
render farms before,

00:01:55.885 --> 00:01:59.270
and he came in and built
out a product on GCP

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:01.550
that took advantage of that
minute-by-minute billing,

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:05.330
the fast boot up times,
the fast shutdown times.

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.110
They could just
simply deploy whatever

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.930
they needed as far
as a render farm was

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:13.280
concerned in under a minute.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.150
So how does Atomic
Fiction use Conductor?

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.750
We use it for
rendering on demand.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:22.990
So submit a job, artist--
as Todd mentioned,

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:24.515
artists are expensive.

00:02:24.515 --> 00:02:26.390
You don't want them
sitting there and waiting

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.210
for any period of time.

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:30.880
So once they submit
their job, we

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.330
want to get that back to
them as quickly as possible.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.180
So especially with
high-priority jobs,

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:40.749
we'll just default map a single
frame to a single instance.

00:02:40.749 --> 00:02:42.790
One of the great things
that we love about Google

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:44.500
is the sheer size of it.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.660
So if we want to
deploy 1,000 instances,

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.960
we can do that in
under 10 minutes.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:53.330
We can then run those for
maybe 1,000 frame sequence,

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:56.550
and then shut those down
with a 10-minute minimum,

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.100
say 11 minutes later.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.750
And then all of this plugs
into the Shotgun's cloud

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.080
solution, which is
a great product.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.891
We love using it.

00:03:07.891 --> 00:03:09.390
We're doing some
deeper integrations

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.260
with Conductor and
Shotgun as well

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:14.160
that will allow us to
feed data back and forth.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.097
We're currently pulling
data out of Shotgun,

00:03:16.097 --> 00:03:17.930
but we'd love to push
some data back into it

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:21.690
around how much does a
shot costs to render?

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.370
How many times have
you rendered it,

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:27.240
and what is the total
cost of that to date?

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.730
And so a little bit about
how Conductor uses you GCP

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:30.960
and why we love it.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.670
So we use Compute Engine
for our render farm,

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:38.930
as I'm sure most of you know.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.580
But the things that we really
like about Compute Engine

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:44.720
are, first and foremost
the permitability.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.900
We were on Amazon
through Zync when

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:48.675
they were on Amazon before.

00:03:48.675 --> 00:03:51.790
And we saw a lot of waste.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:56.670
An average frame for us takes 37
minutes to render using Katana,

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.470
so if we only run that
instance for 37 minutes,

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:03.615
we waste the last 23
minutes of that hour.

00:04:03.615 --> 00:04:06.000
With Google's platform
rollout, we're

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.670
able to run it for
just that 37 minutes,

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:12.170
shut it down, not
worry about it anymore.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:15.460
Next is cloud storage
and persistent disk.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:19.750
So we use cloud storage in
their Nearline archival system

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:23.400
to back up used assets,
to back up old assets,

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.510
to store pretty much
anything that we upload.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:32.010
And it's become, for Atomic,
our single source of truth.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:35.250
The minute we use persistent
disk with a custom NFS set up,

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.740
also some glusters, as
you guys have heard today,

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:39.880
to do our file serving.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:40.900
It works really well.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:42.700
We have yet to hit
a ceiling with it.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:46.830
I'm going to walk through a
pretty detailed example of how

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:50.742
we've done this on our
most recent project,

00:04:50.742 --> 00:04:53.325
and you'll see that we spike it
pretty high a couple of times.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.120
So a little bit about Conductor.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:00.410
So Conductor is a new product.

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:01.750
It's pre-release.

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:06.000
You can go online and sign
up to join the private beta.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:07.000
We're happy to have you.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.959
You're going to get
a phone call from me

00:05:08.959 --> 00:05:10.000
right after that happens.

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:15.060
We designed it with
the artists in mind.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.290
We've gotten a lot of guidance
from the Atomic Fiction team.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.260
It's great to have a
really tight feedback loop

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:23.400
with coming out of a studio.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.740
The artists are really the ones
that we want to cater to here.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:29.890
So we've built tools
to integrate with NUKE,

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:32.520
to integrate with Autodesk
or with Maya, to integrate

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.570
with other products
in the industry,

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.692
V-Ray is another great one.

00:05:37.692 --> 00:05:39.900
And we just want to integrate
it in such a simple way

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:41.816
that an artist can build
their scene and click

00:05:41.816 --> 00:05:43.230
render in the cloud.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:45.730
Send it to Conductor.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:48.200
And then we give those
artists a really simple UI

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.380
so they can go in and
see if jobs are pending,

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:52.600
if they're uploading,
if they're completing,

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:54.150
maybe view some
log files if they

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.890
want to do some of their
own troubleshooting.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:59.480
And then for the render
wranglers and the admins

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.930
out there, we build
tools that make

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:05.100
the troubleshooting process
just that much easier.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.880
So we have complex
job management tools.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:09.600
You can update
priorities on the fly.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:16.160
You can restart, kill, pause,
check the status of jobs.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:17.700
You can build
automated workflows.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:19.930
So as you start to build
priorities and things

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:23.250
into, say, Shotgun
software, you can make sure

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:26.470
when a job goes to the cloud
that a high-priority one drops

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:29.724
right into a queue and
starts going right away.

00:06:29.724 --> 00:06:31.640
And then for the finance
and operations folks,

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:33.140
this is one of the
things that we're

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:37.257
going to talk about in
the next couple of slides.

00:06:37.257 --> 00:06:38.340
It's an interesting thing.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:41.360
Cloud costs have always been
considered really opaque.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.390
And a lot of people have been
concerned about runaway costs,

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:48.900
IT leakage, people
launching instances and not

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:51.330
shutting them down, people
storing stuff and not deleting

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.810
it, and really dealing
with runaway costs.

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:56.430
So one of the things
that we took really

00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:01.930
seriously as we started doing
this was to focus on how do we

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:06.325
want to present that data to the
artist, to the administrators,

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:08.750
to the finance people?

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:10.030
Is it on a daily basis?

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:11.550
Is it real time?

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.500
What we landed on is that
we use Google's BigQuery

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.560
service and Datastore.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.440
So we have all sorts
of bread crumbs

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.120
in our code to be
able to track when

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:24.500
it's launched, what shop
they were associated with,

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.610
what they were doing.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:30.080
And we feed all of that data
into a huge table that we then

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:31.630
can build dashboards
around so you

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:36.782
can see this show
by shot by artist,

00:07:36.782 --> 00:07:38.990
and slice that data in a
lot of different directions.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.660
So I want to talk through
a real-world example here,

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:47.326
because I think that's
probably the best

00:07:47.326 --> 00:07:49.860
way to talk about
cloud costs as compared

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:54.349
with maybe a more traditional
data center approach.

00:07:54.349 --> 00:07:56.140
So the most recent
project that we finished

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:58.860
was Bob Zemeckis's
"The Walk." "The Walk"

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.830
is the story of Philippe Petit
who walked between the Twin

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.420
Towers in the 1970s with
no safety equipment,

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:08.649
for those of you who have not
seen it or heard about it.

00:08:08.649 --> 00:08:11.190
As you would imagine, this took
quite a lot of visual effects

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:14.230
time, because the New York
skyline looks a lot different

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:16.170
than it did in 1970.

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:20.640
So we had to recreate all of
the background, the towers, all

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:25.020
of that for 37 minutes worth of
visual effects for this movie.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:31.220
So it did take quite
a lot of render hours.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:33.900
It took just over
500,000, in fact,

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:36.559
which is 87 years
worth of rendering time

00:08:36.559 --> 00:08:38.330
on a single node.

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:42.830
This is all done on 16 core
instances for our rendering,

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:45.200
32s now that they're out.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:47.225
And we are also doing
a lot of our comping

00:08:47.225 --> 00:08:49.340
now on smaller instances,
taking advantage

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:52.650
of just the full gamut
that Google has to offer.

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:54.310
So you look at our cycle here.

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:56.930
So we started off--
you know, the months

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:59.980
are somewhat arbitrary,
but asset creation, poorly

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:01.590
optimized renders.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:04.200
That's what that early ramp is.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:09.300
Months three, four, and five
are October, November, December.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.010
So that's the holiday dip.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:12.760
People are not working
for half the month.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:14.570
This year was especially.

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:16.800
Everybody had about
two weeks off.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.910
And then you see us ramp
way back up into production

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:24.760
and peak out there with
quite a lot of render hours

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:26.900
in what was March.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:29.360
Then you can see it drops
back down to almost 0.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:31.510
And then this now,
this new month, it

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:33.310
is all the way down
to 0 for this project.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.820
So zooming in on March alone,
because that was really

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:42.150
our big spike month, over
the whole course of the film,

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:44.990
we used 9.1 million core hours.

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:47.380
We're normalizing some
this data into core hours,

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:49.030
because we used difference
instance types and things

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:49.660
like that.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:53.040
But in March alone, we
used almost three million

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:54.990
core hours.

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:58.000
You can see this is
on a day-by-day basis

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.480
the breakdown of
our render process.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.530
So sort of a normal
two-week cycle.

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:05.270
Then you see a big
spike as we started

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:07.690
getting into the final
film reviews with Bob.

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:10.040
And then you see
one big spike, which

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:12.670
was the night before
our big film review,

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:16.180
where we had to render a whole
bunch of shots and scenes

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:19.140
and do a whole bunch of comping
in that particular case.

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:20.380
You see it drops back down.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:22.090
That was the day of the meeting.

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:24.160
We got some notes,
spikes back up.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:28.800
And then it goes down as we
deliver the final project.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:31.150
Coming from the cloud
space for a long time,

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:34.720
this is like the perfect
use case for cloud.

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:37.310
You could own the
infrastructure, but where do

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:38.090
you own for?

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:39.910
Do you own for
the two big spikes

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:43.540
there in the middle and deal
with some delay for the bigger

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:44.190
one?

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:46.610
Well, that would have meant,
in our particular case,

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:48.859
that the shots wouldn't have
been ready for their film

00:10:48.859 --> 00:10:50.020
review at 9:00 AM.

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:52.750
So the cloud is
an obvious choice.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:59.980
So we had a maximum spike
to over 1,000 nodes.

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:01.610
A couple interesting
stats on that.

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:05.370
It took us about 12 minutes
to launch all those nodes.

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:09.890
That was pretty impressive
from my perspective,

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:12.950
when I was watching
it that evening,

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:16.150
watching the number bounce up.

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:19.300
And during our common peaks,
not just in this month

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:22.650
but in other months as well,
we see around 500 nodes.

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:25.430
So for sake of evaluation as
we go through some of the cost

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:32.060
comparisons here, we actually
took it below 500 to 400 nodes

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:35.712
to do a cost comparison,
because at 500, it just

00:11:35.712 --> 00:11:38.170
didn't make any sense to buy
that many if you're only going

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:42.220
to be working on one project.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:44.280
So this is Conductor's
cost curve.

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:48.970
So this is for
Atomic's actual costs.

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:53.120
These are actual rack rate
bills straight out of our system

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:56.010
that we sent to Atomic.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:59.530
You can see that it mimics very
similarly that curve that we

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:01.730
showed in the previous slide.

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:04.070
But you can also see that
the monthly loaded cost

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:09.480
of supporting 400 nodes, for
most of the months there,

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:12.360
it's significantly higher
than what they actually

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:13.740
would have needed.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:15.350
Now, this doesn't
take into account

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:17.308
the fact that we were
working on other projects

00:12:17.308 --> 00:12:19.760
at the same time, and there
is some overlap there.

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:22.310
But if we go back
and we analyze what

00:12:22.310 --> 00:12:25.800
we need, when we need to
hit spikes at the same time,

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:28.470
when we have production
deadlines that overlap,

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:30.720
we think that this is
a pretty fair estimate

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.540
of how much we would
have had to spend

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.960
on an actual infrastructure.

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:43.520
So here are some summary
notes on this example.

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:50.520
We used 9.1 million core
hours, spiked over 1,000 nodes.

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:52.040
With local
infrastructure, if we had

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:54.100
tried to support
some of those spikes

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:56.160
with some
optimizations added, we

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:00.120
would have ended up at
30% average utilization.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:01.400
That's a pretty gross number.

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:03.550
You don't want to ever
see that in a data center,

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:07.550
but it's sort of a reality
of the fairly cyclical work

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:10.390
that we all do.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:13.760
We estimate, by subtracting
those two previous graphs

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:17.500
together or from each other,
we saved about $800,000

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:19.490
on this show alone.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:22.030
That's a pretty big number.

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:25.890
When we look at the bids, that
makes us much more competitive

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:29.590
than some of the other studios
that are out there offering

00:13:29.590 --> 00:13:32.590
similar-style visual effects.

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:34.560
A lot of this we have
to thank Google for.

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:36.830
I mean, billing by the
minute, that's amazing.

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:38.850
We obviously would
not be able to achieve

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:43.890
any of these sorts of numbers
with hourly billing at Amazon.

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:46.440
The hardware is
delivered on demand.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.990
If you compare this to
a three-year buy cycle,

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:51.017
you have to buy
hardware and you're not

00:13:51.017 --> 00:13:52.600
going to be able to
get updates on it.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:55.782
Google updates their
hardware all the time.

00:13:55.782 --> 00:13:57.990
So you're constantly getting
the latest and greatest,

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:00.530
better network performance,
better servers.

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:04.450
As things start to go obsolete,
they're already updating them.

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:05.700
And then the global footprint.

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:07.570
So we have offices
in Montreal that

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:10.256
are doing rendering--
Montreal and Oakland.

00:14:10.256 --> 00:14:11.630
So being able to
have this happen

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:13.046
in the middle of
the country where

00:14:13.046 --> 00:14:15.640
we can do caching between
the two with Avere,

00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:17.950
and allow Google to be
our single source of truth

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:23.090
that we pull any texture,
color, dependency down from

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:25.950
to do work on is amazing.

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:28.130
And the fact that Google's
network is so vast

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:30.305
allows that to happen
really seamlessly.

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:39.300
So I want to wrap up
with a little bit of talk

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:42.170
on where we see the
future of the cloud going.

00:14:42.170 --> 00:14:45.970
We see further democratization
of these resources and easier

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:47.100
access.

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:48.890
We're seeing,
through partnerships

00:14:48.890 --> 00:14:50.610
that Google is
forging, that we're

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:53.260
forging with some of the
vendors in the industry,

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:57.470
that there's an appetite to
do cloud-based licensing.

00:14:57.470 --> 00:15:01.230
So licensing as a service, pay
by the hour, pay by the minute,

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:04.840
pay by the second in some
of our partners' cases.

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:07.270
That's something that
wasn't there before.

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:08.960
So we want to help
lower the barrier

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:11.370
to entry so that people
can get on the cloud

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:14.705
and can set these farms up
and do their work easier.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:22.450
At least in our case, we
already see cost savings.

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:24.660
We know through a lot
of the discussions

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:28.950
that we've had that people have
done a whole lot of analysis

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:32.850
on this, and they don't
think the savings are there.

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:35.380
We believe that they are,
and we're passing those

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:37.500
through to our customers,
or Atomic Fiction

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:39.890
is in their bids.

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:42.212
Our bids are very
cost competitive

00:15:42.212 --> 00:15:44.420
and significantly lower than
a lot of the competition

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:44.920
out there.

00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:48.330
And it allows us to afford
higher quality artists

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:52.110
so the end product ends up
being much higher quality.

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:54.847
And then in the long run, we
do see the entire workflow

00:15:54.847 --> 00:15:55.680
moving to the cloud.

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:59.210
We've already got things like
Shotgun that's cloud-based.

00:15:59.210 --> 00:16:01.730
People are starting to
rendering in the cloud.

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:04.130
But as technologies come
out for better compression

00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:07.140
and for things like
that where we could

00:16:07.140 --> 00:16:09.580
put artists' workstations
in the cloud,

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:12.440
we could put the full
render pipeline up there,

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:17.370
and be able to stream back
color-accurate scenes.

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:19.700
All of a sudden you
think about the industry

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:23.000
and how many people are moving
to Canada and to other areas

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:25.780
due to the tax
benefits, you'd be

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:27.580
able to create a
studio on demand

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:33.080
and simply give all of these
artists Chromebooks or MacBook

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:36.600
Airs with big high-def
monitors that they can use,

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:38.460
and you end up saving
a whole lot of money

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:42.080
on overhead by being
able to do that.

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:46.534
So with that, I'm going to be
around for next hour or so.

00:16:46.534 --> 00:16:49.200
I know that I'm standing between
you guys and a delicious Google

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:50.110
lunch.

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:52.080
So come ask questions.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:52.980
I'm happy to chat.

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:55.910
You can find out more
information about Conductor

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:59.100
by going to renderconductor.com
and signing up for our beta.

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:01.740
Happy to get in touch
and chat with you

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:04.109
about what we're doing.

00:17:04.109 --> 00:17:07.050
And thank you very much.

